.TH SDL_GetEnvironment 3 "SDL 3.5.0" "Simple Directmedia Layer" "SDL3 FUNCTIONS"
.SH NAME
SDL_GetEnvironment \- Get the process environment.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <SDL3/SDL_stdinc.h>
.PP
.BI "SDL_Environment * SDL_GetEnvironment(void);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
This is initialized at application start and is not affected by setenv() and unsetenv() calls after that point\[char46] Use
.BR SDL_SetEnvironmentVariable ()
and
.BR SDL_UnsetEnvironmentVariable ()
if you want to modify this environment, or
.BR SDL_setenv_unsafe ()
or
.BR SDL_unsetenv_unsafe ()
if you want changes to persist in the C runtime environment after
.BR SDL_Quit ()\[char46]
.PP
Note that on Windows, the variable names pulled in from the system at startup have their ASCII values uppercased, to match what most platforms expect even though the Windows system environment table is case-insensitive\[char46] Once those uppercased variable names are in an
.BR SDL_Environment ,
SDL treats them as case-sensitive\[char46]

.SH RETURN VALUE
Returns a pointer to the environment for the process or NULL on failure; call
.BR SDL_GetError ()
for more information\[char46]

.SH THREAD SAFETY
It is safe to call this function from any thread\[char46]

.SH AVAILABILITY
This function is available since SDL 3\[char46]2\[char46]0\[char46]

